    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3536              REVISED 03/04/11\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nauthorizing the lease of lands located at\nthe state university of New York college\nat Farmingdale\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo authorize the State University of New York College at\nFarmingdale to lease lands to expand the Broad Hollow Bioscience\nPark. By doing so, this bill will promote the retention and creation\nof high tech, well paying jobs, and will increase educational\nopportunities for students to intern and potentially start careers in\nthis fast growing sector.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection one contains the legislative findings.\n\nSection two defines Broad Hollow Bioscience Park, Inc. (BHBP).\n\nSection three defines \"project\" and \"project labor agreement\".\n\nSection four authorizes the State University Trustees, without public\nbidding, to lease and otherwise contract to make available to Broad\nHollow Bioscience Park, Inc. a portion of the lands at SUNY\nFarmingdale for the purpose of developing, constructing and operating\na biotechnology and other technology oriented office, research and\nmanufacturing park.\n\nSection five relates to section 15-A of the Executive Law.\n\nSection six states that all work performed on projects authorized by\nthis bill shall be deemed public work and shall be performed in\naccordance with Article 8 of the Labor Law.\n\nSection seven states that the bill will not waive or impair any rights\nor benefits of SUNY employees, and that they shall continue their\nwork as normal.\n\nSection eight allows BHBP to require contractors to enter into a\nproject labor agreement when certain conditions are met. When a\nproject labor agreement is not required, then any contractor,\nsubcontractor, lease, grant, bond, covenant or other agreement for a\nproject shall be awarded pursuant to section 135 of the State Finance\nLaw.\n\nSection nine relates to the grounds and facilities clause of the lease.\n\nSection 10 requires the lease to include an indemnity provision.\n\nSection 11 requires all contracts between BHBP and parties it\ncontracts with to be awarded by competitive process.\n\nSection 12 delineates the metes and bounds of the property to be leased.\n\n\nSection 13 gives SUNY three years to execute the lease authorized by\nthis bill.\n\nSection 14 gives this bill preeminence over any inconsistent laws.\n\nSection 15 states the effective date.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nBroad Hollow Bioscience Park (BHBP) is a not-for-profit\nNew York State corporation. In June 1999, legislation was enacted to\nallow the State University of New York (SUNY) to enter into an\nagreement with BBBP for the development of a bioscience park at SUNY\nFarmingdale. In September 1999, BHBP executed a 30-year operating\nagreement with the State University Construction Fund (SUCF) to\nmanage and operate the facility.\n\nFollowing the execution of that agreement, construction of Building I\nbegan on the campus. In 2002, OSI Pharmaceuticals (OSI) agreed to\nbecome BHBP's anchor tenant, and construction began on a second\nbuilding so that OSI could occupy most of Building I while\nearly-stage companies could continue to be incubated in the\nadditional building. Construction on Building II began in late 2005\non the current site which is supported by the general description of\nthe parcel of land in the bill (1,650 feet by 1,780 feet by 600 feet\nby 3,000 feet).\n\nIn 2009, OSI requested additional space that would have required an\nexpansion of the bioscience park. Before OSI's request could be\nconsidered and acted upon, the company announced that it was leaving\nBHBP and relocating its operations. Subsequently, OSI was acquired by\nAstellas Pharma, which announced its intention to reverse the\ndecision and maintain OSI's operations at BHBP, if possible.\n\nTo retain OSI as the critical anchor tenant and accommodate its most\nrecent requirements, BHBP and the college agreed to a footprint of\n38.5 acres total for BHBP. This acreage will support the company's\nactivities and occupation of both Buildings I and II; it also would\naccommodate a planned additional building which would enable the\nBioscience Park to fulfill its role in the educational mission of the\ncollege as well as continuing to provide economic development through\nthe incubation of early-stage companies in a multi-tenant Building III.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
